00:35:35.540 --> 00:35:38.430
- Oh, nothing in particular.

00:35:38.430 --> 00:35:42.520
- Janet, we're still friends, aren't we?

00:35:42.520 --> 00:35:45.650
- Of course we are, better than ever.

00:35:45.650 --> 00:35:47.930
Nothing like a good joke to bring people together.

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:49.680
- A joke?

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:52.740
- You didn't think I was serious, did you?

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:56.370
- No, of course not.

00:35:56.370 --> 00:36:00.000
- Well, I must be getting home.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:01.200
I'll take you in the car.

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:08.790
You should see the roads.

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:10.830
I almost had to swim back from Aunt Nellie's.

00:36:15.850 --> 00:36:19.350
Uh, Doris, this is Miss Spence.

00:36:20.250 --> 00:36:22.520
You've often heard me talk about her.

00:36:23.310 --> 00:36:24.660
How do you do?

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:26.180
How do you do, Mrs. Maurier?

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:30.000
You know, it sounds too ridiculous for an old woman like me to be called

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:36.750
you there. Do you mind if I call you Doris? I'd love it. And you must call me Janet. Yes,

00:36:36.810 --> 00:36:50.540
Miss Spence. I mean Janet. Isn't she adorable? Well, I'll get my coat. Tell me, Doris, are

00:36:50.540 --> 00:36:59.410
you very, very happy? Yes, I think so. You only think so. No, no, I don't mean that.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:03.200
I'm sorry. Let's talk about something else if it upsets you.

00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:07.060
But I am happy, really and truly. It's just that...

00:37:07.060 --> 00:37:09.240
Well, I'm not very clever.

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:12.240
Henry seems to know everything you know about art and things.

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:18.050
Art and things. Oh, you sweet child.

00:37:18.050 --> 00:37:20.320
Well, here we are. I shan't be long, dear.

00:37:20.320 --> 00:37:22.320
Henry, please let me go with you.

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:24.320
No, no. You stay here and change your shoes.

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:25.250
But I don't want to.

00:37:25.250 --> 00:37:27.210
What's the matter? Afraid of the dark?

00:37:27.210 --> 00:37:29.190
No. Afraid of something else.

00:37:30.250 --> 00:37:32.250
Can't you feel it, Henry? You must be very insensitive.

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:33.250
Feel what?

00:37:33.250 --> 00:37:35.460
She's still here, isn't she, Doris?

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:40.270
All right, come on. Let's go to the car.

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:43.960
Well, well, well. Married, eh? Sensible fellow.

00:37:43.960 --> 00:37:47.200
High time this girl got married, too. You give her a talking tomorrow.

00:37:47.200 --> 00:37:48.200
Father.

00:37:48.200 --> 00:37:49.200
I'll do my best.

00:37:49.200 --> 00:37:50.060
Well, where is she?

00:37:50.060 --> 00:37:53.770
Take off that ridiculous hat.

00:37:53.770 --> 00:37:56.520
That's better.

00:37:56.520 --> 00:38:00.000
It's your mother when we were first engaged the living image of her.

00:38:00.760 --> 00:38:04.230
- Turn around. - Yes, definitely so.

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:06.820
Remember the picture of her in the riding habit?

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:09.350
That's the thing she was wearing when I saw her first.

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:11.520
Don't you ever ride in anything but a habit, my dear?

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:13.570
Women aren't the right shape for bridges.

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:17.850
Whereas in a riding habit, well, man could still have illusions.

00:38:18.280 --> 00:38:20.360
And what's life without illusions?

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:23.400
Nasty, solitary, brutish and short.

00:38:23.520 --> 00:38:25.700
Women's legs are shorter even than life.

00:38:26.660 --> 00:38:29.030
Let's see you without that hideous Macintosh.

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:35.360
What a charming brooch.

00:38:38.090 --> 00:38:39.030
Henry gave it to me.

00:38:39.250 --> 00:38:40.510
Well, we really have to run.

00:38:41.170 --> 00:38:42.450
Got to be in London tonight,

00:38:42.590 --> 00:38:44.690
and then we're taking the early boat train in the morning.

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:45.430
Goodbye, General.

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:46.450
Goodbye, my dear father.

00:38:46.550 --> 00:38:46.750
Good night.

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:53.560
Good night.

00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:30.000
Well, I'm glad poor Mrs. Morier wasn't here to see what has happened to her brooch.

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:35.130
- Your brooch, really. - I was a fool to think I'd ever get it.

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:38.050
You don't get diamonds for friendship.

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:40.800
He told me he'd known the girl for several months.

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:43.810
That means that even while poor Emily was alive.

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:46.290
Pigs, that's what they are, every one of them.

00:39:46.290 --> 00:39:49.410
I tell you, they've got no shame, no decent feelings.

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:52.090
And now, when she's scarcely cold in her grave...

00:39:52.090 --> 00:39:54.990
Must have been a great relief to him.

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:57.370
You mean when she died?

00:39:57.370 --> 00:39:59.190
Seeing that he wanted to marry the girl.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:06.330
tells you he didn't have to marry her i'd be ready to bet on it then it was lucky for him when poor

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:15.130
emily died when she did just at the right time just at the right time miss spence you don't suppose

00:40:15.130 --> 00:40:28.070
suppose what why wouldn't he let me give her her medicine well you're not suggesting but that's

00:40:28.070 --> 00:40:30.000
ridiculous stuff he brought back from the

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:32.600
- What stuff? - Stuff for killing weeds.

00:40:32.680 --> 00:40:35.320
I happened to look at the label. I know what was in it.

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:41.390
Why, he made all that fuss about those red currants just to give himself an alibi.

00:40:41.470 --> 00:40:43.710
- You're not serious, are you? - I certainly am.

00:40:43.790 --> 00:40:45.590
You're mad. It's unthinkable.

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:47.330
After all, I've known him for years. He couldn't...

00:40:47.410 --> 00:40:52.650
You've known A, Mr. Maurier, the one who talks so nicely about pictures and all that sort of thing.

00:40:52.730 --> 00:40:56.050
But you've never known the one who can't keep his hands off girls.

00:40:56.130 --> 00:40:58.480
He'd do anything, I tell you, anything.

00:41:00.410 --> 00:41:02.790
Why did it happen on the day I was out?

00:41:02.790 --> 00:41:05.190
- What difference did that make? - What difference?

00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:08.610
I've seen these cases, I'd have recognized the symptoms immediately.

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:10.310
So what does he do?

00:41:10.310 --> 00:41:14.010
Choose as a day when he knows I won't be back until late.

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:15.270
'Til it's all over, in fact.

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:18.930
And then he goes out himself, on the tiles most likely, with that girl of his.

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:21.390
- No, he wouldn't do that. - Oh, yes, he would.

00:41:21.390 --> 00:41:25.660
And when he comes home, he turns on me and says I killed her with those red currants.

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:27.460
Currants indeed.

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:30.000
After all, Dr. Liver thought it might have been the currants.

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:33.390
Yes. And why? Because the other one keeps harping on it.

00:41:33.720 --> 00:41:35.220
And so I have to take the blame.

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:37.720
I'm the scapegoat. I'm the one to be crucified.

00:41:37.720 --> 00:41:40.690
I tell you I'm not going to put up with it any longer.

00:41:40.690 --> 00:41:42.690
And I'm not thinking only of myself.

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:44.190
It's a matter of principle.

00:41:44.190 --> 00:41:45.690
I want to see justice done.

00:41:45.690 --> 00:41:48.190
I want the whole world to know the truth.

00:41:48.190 --> 00:41:50.110
You talk as though you knew it yourself?

00:41:50.110 --> 00:41:51.490
I do.

00:41:51.490 --> 00:41:55.400
I'm as certain of it now as I shall be after they've had the post-mortem.

00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:57.010
The post-mortem?

00:41:57.010 --> 00:41:58.890
Yes. You know what that is, don't you?

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:01.540
You mean to speak to Dr. Livert about it?

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:02.560
Dr. Livert?

00:42:02.780 --> 00:42:03.640
No, of course not.

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:05.320
He wouldn't want to admit he'd made a mistake.

00:42:05.500 --> 00:42:05.720
No.

00:42:06.380 --> 00:42:07.530
I know who to go to.

00:42:08.060 --> 00:42:09.180
I know what I have to do.

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:27.490
Horrible.

00:42:28.120 --> 00:42:29.680
Digging up somebody after they're dead.

00:42:30.250 --> 00:42:32.050
Just because there's some spiteful gossip.

00:42:33.170 --> 00:42:35.950
That beastly nurse of yours, I can't understand why you keep her.

00:42:36.130 --> 00:42:37.550
Now, dear, don't be unreasonable.

00:42:39.230 --> 00:42:40.910
You know quite well I wanted to send her away,

00:42:40.970 --> 00:42:42.050
but Henry wouldn't hear of it.

00:42:42.430 --> 00:42:43.330
Nor would Dr. Libbert.

00:42:43.910 --> 00:42:45.770
Sending her away would have meant we took her seriously,

00:42:45.890 --> 00:42:47.670
and that's the last impression we want to give.

00:42:49.350 --> 00:42:51.530
We were having such a wonderful time in Paris.

00:42:52.500 --> 00:42:54.120
Then to be called back for this nonsense.

00:42:54.490 --> 00:42:55.900
And the painter's still in the house.

00:42:56.510 --> 00:42:57.710
Horrible smell everywhere.

00:42:58.360 --> 00:43:00.000
Darling, how dreadfully unkind.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:05.730
of me i quite forgot to ask you how you've been is everything going as it should well i still feel

00:43:05.730 --> 00:43:11.490
sick in the morning if that's what you mean and liberty's pleased with you is he seems to be it

00:43:11.490 --> 00:43:20.010
must be a strange wonderful feeling you mean to be going to have a baby if you ask my opinion i

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:24.720
think it's awful it'll be all right when the baby's actually there but right now i tell you

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:29.860
i'd rather have the measles again at least it doesn't last so long will you nurse the baby yourself

00:43:30.860 --> 00:43:32.560
I don't know. I hadn't thought about it.

00:43:32.560 --> 00:43:36.690
I would if I had one. I wouldn't feel it were really mine if I didn't.

00:43:36.690 --> 00:43:38.450
When do you expect Henry?

00:43:38.450 --> 00:43:40.050
He ought to be back pretty soon.

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:41.910
How was he when he went off this morning?

00:43:41.910 --> 00:43:43.610
Rather worried, I suppose.

00:43:43.610 --> 00:43:45.560
No, he was too angry to be worried.

00:43:45.560 --> 00:43:49.180
Makes him furious the way they're treating him.

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:53.910
Then you do not agree with Dr. Libard's diagnosis as to the cause of death?

00:43:53.910 --> 00:43:55.420
Yes and no.

00:43:55.420 --> 00:44:00.000
I am of the opinion that Dr. Libard was perfectly correct in stating that death was due to

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:04.150
Where I differ from him is in regard to the cause.

00:44:04.210 --> 00:44:07.070
And what was the cause, in your opinion, Dr. Dawson?

00:44:07.130 --> 00:44:08.070
Arsenic, sir.

00:44:08.130 --> 00:44:09.720
But that's impossible.

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:11.720
No interruptions, please.

00:44:11.780 --> 00:44:15.640
You'll be given an opportunity of speaking later on, Mr. Maurier.

00:44:15.700 --> 00:44:17.930
Please go on, Dr. Dawson.

00:44:17.960 --> 00:44:19.900
The organs were removed and examined.

00:44:19.910 --> 00:44:21.750
Both Ranch's and Marsh's tests were used.

00:44:21.810 --> 00:44:24.800
And the presence of poison in considerable quantities

00:44:24.860 --> 00:44:26.800
was clearly established.

00:44:26.860 --> 00:44:29.670
And were the quantities sufficient to...

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:36.060
constitute a fatal dose? Unquestionably. Do you know Mr. Morier by sight? Yes, sir. Do you think you

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:41.560
could give him this note? It's rather important. All right, sir. I'll manage somehow. Thank you.

00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:49.760
Much obliged, sir. It was one of your duties, was it not, to bring Mrs. Morier's medicine to her

00:44:49.760 --> 00:45:00.000
after meals? It was. Did you bring it to her after lunch on the day that she died? No, I did not. I'll

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:02.680
Yes, sir. Then, while the next one's called, I'll slip in and give it to him.

00:45:02.760 --> 00:45:06.150
Take the testament in your right hand. Will you repeat the oath?

00:45:06.260 --> 00:45:10.240
I swear by Almighty God that the evidence I shall give the court

00:45:10.320 --> 00:45:13.600
shall be the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth.

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:15.780
So help me, God.

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:22.500
So I took the coffee-things out into the garden and put them down on the table.

00:45:22.870 --> 00:45:24.200
And what then?

00:45:24.610 --> 00:45:28.740
Mrs. Morier says to me, "My medicine, Clara, run and fetch it."

00:45:28.840 --> 00:45:30.000
And did you go?

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:31.560
No, sir.

00:45:31.560 --> 00:45:32.580
Why not?

00:45:32.580 --> 00:45:36.560
Because Mr Morier says, "Don't bother, Clara. I've got to go in and get a cigar anyway."

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:40.210
Thank you. You may step down.

00:45:41.150 --> 00:45:43.510
And adjourn until 2:30 tomorrow.

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:47.220
The court will adjourn until 2:30 tomorrow afternoon.

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:06.180
It's too terrible.

00:46:06.710 --> 00:46:08.970
Yes, it's terrible because it's impossible.

00:46:09.430 --> 00:46:10.540
And yet it's happened.

00:46:11.640 --> 00:46:13.240
It's all my fault.

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:15.180
I oughtn't to have let you love me.

00:46:16.150 --> 00:46:17.370
I knew we shouldn't have done it.

00:46:18.250 --> 00:46:20.190
But I cared for you so much.

00:46:21.630 --> 00:46:23.850
Darling, if they do anything to you, I shall kill myself.

00:46:23.910 --> 00:46:24.950
Don't talk nonsense.

00:46:39.630 --> 00:46:42.580
Why did you do it, Henry? Why did you do it?

00:46:42.680 --> 00:46:58.060
People seem to take it for granted that I murdered my wife.

00:47:01.530 --> 00:47:03.900
Do I look like the sort of man who goes about

00:47:03.900 --> 00:47:05.570
slaughtering people?

00:47:05.570 --> 00:47:10.250
Finger, neck, and the devil-taker?

00:47:10.250 --> 00:47:12.430
I suppose we imagine I was so insanely in love with you

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:14.950
that I would do anything, anything.

00:47:14.950 --> 00:47:17.580
When will you women understand that all one asks for

00:47:17.580 --> 00:47:22.360
is a little amusement and a quiet life?

00:47:22.360 --> 00:47:25.550
Instead of which, I don't know why the devil

00:47:25.550 --> 00:47:27.990
I ever married you.

00:47:27.990 --> 00:47:30.000
Why did any man in his right mind ever marry

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:31.180
any woman for that matter.

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:35.410
Henry!

00:47:35.760 --> 00:47:55.040
I've just been sent for

00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:55.740
to your house.

00:47:55.900 --> 00:47:56.280
What for?

00:47:56.500 --> 00:47:57.220
Your maid told me

00:47:57.220 --> 00:47:57.620
over the phone

00:47:57.620 --> 00:47:58.100
that your wife

00:47:58.100 --> 00:47:58.760
had taken poison.

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:00.000
Taken poison?

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:02.820
Fortunately, it's nothing more than an overdose of sleeping tablets.

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:05.820
I told them what emergency measures to take, so I'm hoping it won't be too serious.

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:06.870
Get it.

00:48:06.870 --> 00:48:16.890
Is it the result of what happened at the inquest?

00:48:16.890 --> 00:48:18.770
Yes.

00:48:18.770 --> 00:48:20.790
Yes, that and other things.

00:48:20.790 --> 00:48:24.030
I got angry because she took it for granted that I have...

00:48:24.030 --> 00:48:27.280
Well, that I was responsible for what happened to Emily.

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:30.000
I said a little thing.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:43.160
things I shouldn't have said. I see. Libbert, do you think I did it? No, I don't. Then how did it

00:48:43.160 --> 00:48:51.450
happen? One of them tries to kill herself. Perhaps the other succeeded. But why? Why? Ask yourself,

00:48:51.570 --> 00:48:55.700
Morier. If you were a woman, would you have been very happy as Mrs. Henry Morier?

00:49:03.520 --> 00:49:05.260
- I'd rather you didn't come up.

00:49:05.260 --> 00:49:16.160
- Very well.

00:49:16.160 --> 00:49:17.930
- That's that, young woman.

00:49:17.930 --> 00:49:20.210
Two days in bed and you can do what you want.

00:49:20.210 --> 00:49:22.730
No more of this sort of thing, remember?

00:49:22.730 --> 00:49:24.930
- What's to prevent me?

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:27.760
- Nothing except your own common sense and common decency.

00:49:27.760 --> 00:49:30.000
- He doesn't love me, I don't want to go on living.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:34.500
who cares what you want who's interested in your beastly little emotions well i think of somebody

00:49:34.500 --> 00:49:39.220
else for change i think of henry all the time no you don't you think of yourself in relation

00:49:39.220 --> 00:49:46.200
to henry which is a very different proposition thank you macy you may go remember if you wake

00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:54.470
up in the night with cramps in your intestines don't blame henry it's your own fault entirely

00:49:54.470 --> 00:50:00.000
there you're quite right dr liver it was wrong

00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:02.310
I promise I won't do it again.

00:50:03.250 --> 00:50:03.950
Good girl.

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:05.770
No, no, I'm not good.

00:50:06.460 --> 00:50:07.900
That's why all this is happening.

00:50:10.170 --> 00:50:11.860
Tell me, how can I help?

00:50:12.620 --> 00:50:17.220
Well, in the first place, you must believe in him.

00:50:17.860 --> 00:50:20.140
Through thick and thin, in spite of everything.

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:20.940
That's the first thing.

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:26.180
Then whatever happens, you must be strong and calm.

00:50:26.260 --> 00:50:27.680
No tears, no harrowing scenes.

00:50:27.820 --> 00:50:29.340
They're just an indulgence, that's all.

00:50:30.920 --> 00:50:32.960
Some women cry as easily as a pig grunts.

00:50:34.960 --> 00:50:36.420
They enjoy it very nearly as much.

00:50:39.750 --> 00:50:40.430
So don't do it.

00:50:41.590 --> 00:50:42.250
Don't do it.

00:50:43.580 --> 00:50:45.520
And finally remember you're going to have a baby.

00:50:48.560 --> 00:50:50.660
That's probably the best thing that ever happened to Henry.

00:50:50.900 --> 00:50:53.400
So for goodness sake, don't let's make a mess of it.

00:50:55.670 --> 00:50:56.890
See who's here?

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:03.560
Is she all right?

00:51:03.940 --> 00:51:04.340
Flourishing.

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:07.800
And there isn't going to be any more of this sort of nonsense, is there, Doris?

00:51:08.060 --> 00:51:09.730
I'm so thankful you got here in time.

00:51:09.970 --> 00:51:12.470
Yes, but it would have been better if there hadn't been any need for me to come.

00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:14.260
Bye, Doris.

00:51:15.690 --> 00:51:16.150
Bye, Henry.

00:51:16.450 --> 00:51:17.090
Thank you.

00:51:32.460 --> 00:51:33.740
Can you forgive me, Doris?

00:51:34.390 --> 00:51:36.210
Darling, I'm the one who needs to be forgiven.

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:39.220
It was all selfishness, really.

00:51:39.740 --> 00:51:40.720
I see it now.

00:51:41.180 --> 00:51:43.780
I was trying to spite you, trying to get my own face.

00:51:45.690 --> 00:51:46.680
I began it, I'm afraid.

00:51:47.240 --> 00:51:48.340
I ought to have known better.

00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:50.570
At 18?

00:51:51.360 --> 00:51:54.210
I think I tried to kill myself.

00:51:55.470 --> 00:51:58.080
And everything so beautiful, so mysterious.

00:52:00.160 --> 00:52:01.600
Even that fly on the ceiling.

00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:05.090
Even that silly old giant ornaments.

