01:19:35.130 --> 01:19:41.050
- They proved it. - The jury thought they proved it, but do you?

01:19:41.250 --> 01:19:55.670
Of course, you know the basic reason why poor Emily was so dreadfully unhappy.

01:19:55.870 --> 01:20:00.000
What was it?

01:20:00.030 --> 01:20:03.900
That's because she wouldn't accept the facts as she found them.

01:20:04.040 --> 01:20:05.680
If she was an invalid, she'd lost her looks.

01:20:06.850 --> 01:20:09.630
She wanted to be treated as though she were young and pretty.

01:20:10.700 --> 01:20:13.980
What has that got to do with me?

01:20:16.350 --> 01:20:17.530
That is for you to answer.

01:20:19.270 --> 01:20:23.130
I'm only pointing out that it's possible to come to terms even with the most terrible facts.

01:20:25.780 --> 01:20:29.190
Old age, sickness...

01:20:30.800 --> 01:20:35.100
Death. Yes, even with one's own wickedness and folly.

01:20:36.060 --> 01:20:38.270
Those are just words, that's all.

01:20:39.630 --> 01:20:40.310
Just words.

01:20:41.910 --> 01:20:43.810
But they can always be translated into actions.

01:20:48.920 --> 01:20:54.890
Listen, Janet, I can quite understand you're not wanting to go to sleep until you feel that you're safe.

01:20:56.740 --> 01:20:58.840
But did you ever stop to analyze the word?

01:21:00.760 --> 01:21:06.890
safe from what? Safe in which respect? One can shut the door against one danger and be wide open

01:21:06.890 --> 01:21:19.550
to another. Look at yourself. You want to be safe from death. At what price? The price of feeling

01:21:19.550 --> 01:21:30.000
guilty. The price of being driven mad by the sense of guilt. And what then? In your madness? Won't you try to

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:39.410
do away with yourself, then again, that's death. You run away from death, and what do

01:21:39.410 --> 01:21:52.810
you run into? Death, madness, and death. But if you don't run away, if you face the facts,

01:21:52.870 --> 01:21:58.300
if you accept your destiny, there's something like a certainty that you will escape madness.

01:22:00.160 --> 01:22:02.240
A very good chance of escaping death.

01:22:06.950 --> 01:22:10.220
Well, I'll think about it.

01:22:12.190 --> 01:22:16.000
I'm terribly thirsty.

01:22:16.800 --> 01:22:17.620
Would you like a drink?

01:22:18.540 --> 01:22:20.860
Yes, that's a good idea.

01:22:21.150 --> 01:22:22.190
I'll get one for you.

01:23:05.280 --> 01:23:06.120
Kali, isn't it?

01:23:07.250 --> 01:23:08.630
The great mother.

01:23:10.270 --> 01:23:13.530
Precisely because she's a mother, she's also the goddess of destruction.

01:23:14.670 --> 01:23:16.710
You give life, you also give death.

01:23:18.540 --> 01:23:19.020
Inevitably.

01:23:20.690 --> 01:23:23.850
I must say they had a pretty realistic view of the world, these old Hindus.

01:23:26.650 --> 01:23:28.040
Oh, I beg your pardon.

01:23:29.240 --> 01:23:30.000
I'm really most awful.

01:23:30.030 --> 01:23:31.620
I'm awfully sorry.

01:23:31.620 --> 01:23:34.150
I don't think it'll harm the carpet, do you?

01:23:34.150 --> 01:23:40.380
Just a little fizzy water and a spot of alcohol?

01:23:40.380 --> 01:23:41.450
Do I get another?

01:23:41.450 --> 01:23:42.500
Let me do it for you.

01:23:42.500 --> 01:23:47.840
No, no, let me.

01:23:47.840 --> 01:23:51.830
That's Emily's place, isn't it?

01:23:51.830 --> 01:23:54.600
Yes.

01:23:54.600 --> 01:24:00.000
I thought I recognized it.

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:06.560
I'm rather grotesque when one comes to think of it.

01:24:06.640 --> 01:24:10.520
Executing a man for murdering a dead woman.

01:24:10.600 --> 01:24:14.400
Murdering a dead woman? What do you mean?

01:24:14.480 --> 01:24:17.160
Well, that's what she was.

01:24:17.240 --> 01:24:20.780
Two months, three months, four months at the outside.

01:24:20.860 --> 01:24:26.790
That's all she had in front of her.

01:24:26.870 --> 01:24:30.000
You mean...

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:34.760
If she hadn't been poisoned, she would have been dead in two or three months.

01:24:34.760 --> 01:24:47.030
That's it. What are you laughing at?

01:25:26.040 --> 01:25:30.000
- How did you do that yesterday? - I did it.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:44.110
yesterday. Let me tell you something. It happened to me last night. Quite suddenly, in the middle

01:25:44.110 --> 01:25:53.990
of a paroxysm of rage and despair, it was like actually hearing your voice. Only there weren't

01:25:53.990 --> 01:25:59.250
any words. There was just a kind of absolute certainty.

01:26:01.240 --> 01:26:01.800
A certainty.

01:26:02.920 --> 01:26:05.930
I knew that everything was finally all right.

01:26:06.050 --> 01:26:06.670
I knew it.

01:26:09.870 --> 01:26:13.390
Of course, one's got to face things as they really are.

01:26:14.570 --> 01:26:17.510
One's got to forget what one would like them to be.

01:26:22.430 --> 01:26:25.970
Tell me, my darling, have the leaves started to turn?

01:26:27.210 --> 01:26:28.910
They're all golden now.

01:26:29.370 --> 01:26:29.940
You're on the beach?

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:34.880
I remember when I was a little boy, walking in the beech woods.

01:26:35.770 --> 01:26:42.800
I used to pretend that dry leaves were heaps of money, knee-deep in gold, like the Count

01:26:42.800 --> 01:27:00.000
of Monte Cristo, accepted to command.

01:27:00.540 --> 01:27:02.100
But I can't accept it, I can't.

01:27:03.890 --> 01:27:05.330
Shall I tell you what was hardest?

01:27:08.120 --> 01:27:10.760
Accepting the fact that I shouldn't ever see you again.

01:27:11.850 --> 01:27:13.070
I wish I were dead.

01:27:13.750 --> 01:27:15.130
I wish I'd never been born.

01:27:15.710 --> 01:27:17.450
There's nothing to do with one's wishes.

01:27:17.940 --> 01:27:21.380
After all, we did not ask to be born, but we've got to put up with life.

01:27:21.760 --> 01:27:28.710
Even if we don't like it, even if we can't understand it, and mind you, we can never understand it while we are actually living it.

01:27:30.250 --> 01:27:40.370
has to be lived forward, understood backwards. So, there it is. That's what I suddenly understood.

01:27:40.370 --> 01:27:46.910
Meanwhile, what was I doing? The exact opposite of what had to be done. Knocking my hands

01:27:46.910 --> 01:27:54.030
to pieces, driving myself mad, raving against the injustice of the thing. But it is unjust.

01:27:54.030 --> 01:27:58.250
From the outside, yes, from other people's point of view. But you know, if you accept

01:27:58.250 --> 01:28:00.000
the injustice that's been done to us, you know what to do?

01:28:01.600 --> 01:28:12.670
If you say to yourself, well, this is what has happened, and I put up with it, I actually will it with this.

01:28:13.560 --> 01:28:19.100
In some strange, mysterious way, the nightmare makes a kind of sense.

01:28:19.200 --> 01:28:22.270
I know it's difficult to explain, but it's true.

01:28:23.670 --> 01:28:26.740
